drm/mediatek: defer the bind only when the DT actually promises an IOMMU

Mainline returns -EPROBE_DEFER from mtk_drm_bind() whenever
iommu_present() is false, because mediatek-drm cannot scan out scattered
buffers without one. drm/0010 replaced that with a warning, correctly:
while mainline mtk-iommu had no MT6797 support no IOMMU would ever
register, the bind would defer forever, and the machine would have no
display at all.

With an M4U in the tree that warning is now wrong in the other direction,
and the margin is small. Measured on kernel #34:

  [0.837] mediatek-drm: no IOMMU, using contiguous CMA buffers
  [0.886] mtk-iommu 10205000.iommu: bound 14020000.larb

Fifty milliseconds. DRM binds first and comes up on CMA regardless of any
iommus properties the display components carry -- and it does so
silently, so the result would read as "the IOMMU did not help" rather
than "the IOMMU was not there yet". That is a nasty class of bug to chase
because both look identical from userspace.

Asking the device tree settles it without reintroducing the hang: if an
enabled m4u node exists then an IOMMU is coming and waiting is right; if
there is none, nothing is coming and waiting would be fatal.
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_drm_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/mediatek/mtk_drm_drv.c
@@ -649,15 +649,43 @@
 	int ret, i;
 
 	/*
-	 * GEMINI (B-13 follow-on): mainline mtk-iommu has no MT6797 support,
-	 * so no IOMMU ever registers on the platform bus and the stock
-	 * iommu_present() gate would defer this bind forever. The MT6797
-	 * bring-up runs the SMI larbs in MMU-bypass mode and uses physically
-	 * contiguous CMA buffers instead, which the mtk_drm GEM code handles
-	 * fine via dma_alloc_attrs(). Warn rather than defer.
+	 * GEMINI: defer if and only if the device tree promises an IOMMU.
+	 *
+	 * Mainline returns -EPROBE_DEFER whenever iommu_present() is false,
+	 * because mediatek-drm cannot scan out scattered buffers without one.
+	 * That was wrong here for as long as mainline mtk-iommu had no MT6797
+	 * support: no IOMMU would ever register, the bind would defer forever,
+	 * and the machine would have no display at all. So it was changed to a
+	 * warning, and the bring-up ran the SMI larbs in MMU-bypass mode with
+	 * physically contiguous CMA buffers.
+	 *
+	 * With an M4U now in the tree, an unconditional warning is wrong in the
+	 * other direction. Probe order is against us and it is close: measured
+	 * on kernel #34, mediatek-drm binds at 0.837 s and mtk-iommu binds larb0
+	 * at 0.886 s. Bind fifty milliseconds early and DRM comes up on CMA,
+	 * silently, no matter what iommus properties the display components
+	 * carry -- which would read as "the IOMMU did not help" rather than
+	 * "the IOMMU was not there yet".
+	 *
+	 * Asking the device tree settles it without reintroducing the hang: if
+	 * an enabled m4u node exists then an IOMMU is coming and waiting is
+	 * correct; if it does not, nothing is coming and waiting would be fatal.
 	 */
-	if (!iommu_present(&platform_bus_type))
-		dev_warn(dev, "no IOMMU, using contiguous CMA buffers\n");
+	if (!iommu_present(&platform_bus_type)) {
+		struct device_node *iommu_np;
+		bool iommu_expected;
+
+		iommu_np = of_find_compatible_node(NULL, NULL,
+						   "mediatek,mt6797-m4u");
+		iommu_expected = iommu_np && of_device_is_available(iommu_np);
+		of_node_put(iommu_np);
+
+		if (iommu_expected) {
+			dev_info(dev, "waiting for the M4U the DT promises\n");
+			return -EPROBE_DEFER;
+		}
+		dev_warn(dev, "no IOMMU and none declared, using contiguous CMA buffers\n");
+	}
 
 	pdev = of_find_device_by_node(private->mutex_node);
 	if (!pdev) {
